        BookChapter(
            id = 8,
            partTitle = "Part III: What Jesus Said by Himself",
            title = "'He Who Has Seen Me Has Seen the Father'",
            subtitle = "The Visible Image of the Invisible God",
            keyVerseRef = "John 14:9",
            keyVerseText = "Jesus said to him, 'Have I been with you so long, and yet you have not known Me, Philip? He who has seen Me has seen the Father; so how can you say, \"Show us the Father\"?'",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "On the night before His crucifixion, in the Upper Room, Jesus spoke with deep intimacy to His disciples. He revealed: 'If you had known Me, you would have known My Father also; and from now on you know Him and have seen Him' (John 14:7). Philip, eager for a visible theophany like Moses or Isaiah experienced, asked: 'Lord, show us the Father, and it is sufficient for us.'",
                "Jesus's response is one of the most tender yet monumental assertions in Holy Scripture: 'Have I been with you so long, and yet you have not known Me, Philip? He who has seen Me has seen the Father; so how can you say, \"Show us the Father\"? Do you not believe that I am in the Father, and the Father in Me?'",
                "No angel, prophet, or created being in heaven or earth could ever utter such words. Moses had to hide in the cleft of the rock because no man could see God and live. Yet Jesus reveals that to look upon Him is to look upon the very character, essence, love, and reality of God Almighty. As Colossians 2:9 proclaims: 'For in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily.'"
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("John 14:9", "He who has seen Me has seen the Father; so how can you say, 'Show us the Father'?", "Seen the Father", "Christ is the perfect, radiant reflection of the Father's glory."),
                ScriptureVerse("Colossians 1:15", "He is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation.", "Image (Eikon)", "The visible manifestation of the unseen God."),
                ScriptureVerse("Hebrews 1:3", "Who being the brightness of His glory and the express image of His person, and upholding all things by the word of His power...", "Express image of His person", "Greek 'charakter' — the exact representation of God's being.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "Jesus is the living self-revelation of God; to see Christ in His holiness, love, and power is to see the Father Himself.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Charakter (χαρακτήρ)" to "The exact imprint, representation, or stamp of essence.",
                "Eikon (εἰκών)" to "Living manifestation and visible image of the unseen.",
                "Pleroma (πλήρωμα)" to "The entire fullness of divine attributes dwelling bodily in Jesus."
            )
        ),
        BookChapter(
            id = 9,
            partTitle = "Part III: What Jesus Said by Himself",
            title = "Exercising the Exclusive Prerogatives of God",
            subtitle = "Forgiving Sins, Raising the Dead, and Receiving Worship",
            keyVerseRef = "Mark 2:5, 10–11 & John 20:28",
            keyVerseText = "When Jesus saw their faith, He said to the paralytic, 'Son, your sins are forgiven you.' ... 'That you may know that the Son of Man has power on earth to forgive sins' ... And Thomas answered and said to Him, 'My Lord and my God!'",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "It is not only what Jesus claimed with words, but what He did with uncontested authority that proves His divinity. In Capernaum, four men lowered a paralytic through the roof. Seeing their faith, Jesus announced: 'Son, your sins are forgiven you' (Mark 2:5). The scribes rightly reasoned in their hearts: 'Why does this Man speak blasphemies like this? Who can forgive sins but God alone?'",
                "The scribes' theological premise was 100% correct: only the God against whom all sins are committed has the authority to pardon sins! To prove He possessed that divine prerogative, Jesus healed the paralyzed man instantaneously with a single word. Furthermore, Jesus stated that just as the Father raises the dead, 'even so the Son gives life to whom He will' (John 5:21).",
                "Most decisively, Jesus accepted religious worship ('proskuneo'). When the Apostle John fell down before an angel in Revelation 19:10 and 22:9, the angel panicked: 'See that you do not do that! I am your fellow servant... Worship God!' When Cornelius fell down before Peter (Acts 10:25-26), Peter pulled him up: 'Stand up; I myself am also a man.' But when Thomas fell before the risen Jesus and confessed: 'My Lord and my God!' (John 20:28), Jesus did not rebuke him—He blessed him!"
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Mark 2:7, 10", "'Why does this Man speak blasphemies like this? Who can forgive sins but God alone?' ... 'That you may know that the Son of Man has power on earth to forgive sins...'", "Who can forgive sins but God alone?", "Jesus proves His divine identity by exercising the prerogative of forgiving sins."),
                ScriptureVerse("John 20:28–29", "And Thomas answered and said to Him, 'My Lord and my God!' Jesus said to him, 'Thomas, because you have seen Me, you have believed. Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed.'", "My Lord and my God! (Ho Kyrios mou kai ho Theos mou)", "The climactic confession of the Gospel of John, received and validated by Christ."),
                ScriptureVerse("Matthew 14:33", "Then those who were in the boat came and worshiped Him, saying, 'Truly You are the Son of God.'", "Worshiped Him (prosekunesan)", "Jesus freely accepted worship after walking on the stormy sea.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "Jesus performed divine acts (forgiving sins, raising the dead, commanding creation) and accepted the highest form of worship reserved for God alone.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Proskuneo (προσκυνέω)" to "Prostrating oneself in religious adoration and worship.",
                "Ho Theos mou (ὁ Θεός μου)" to "Greek nominative used as vocative: 'My very God!'",
                "Exousia (ἐξουσία)" to "Inherent divine authority and power to forgive and execute judgment."
            )
        ),
        BookChapter(
            id = 10,
            partTitle = "Part IV: Comparing What John Saw and What the Prophets Saw",
            title = "John 12:41: Isaiah Saw Jesus's Glory",
            subtitle = "The Apostolic Key Unlocking Old Testament Theophanies",
            keyVerseRef = "John 12:41",
            keyVerseText = "These things Isaiah said because he saw His glory and spoke of Him.",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "One of the most astonishing verses in the entire New Testament is found tucked inside the twelfth chapter of the Gospel of John. The Apostle John was reflecting on why so many people remained in unbelief despite the miraculous signs Jesus had performed. To explain this hardness of heart, John quoted from two famous chapters of Isaiah: Isaiah 53:1 ('Lord, who has believed our report?') and Isaiah 6:10 ('He has blinded their eyes and hardened their hearts').",
                "Immediately after quoting Isaiah's vision from Isaiah 6—where Isaiah saw the LORD of hosts seated upon the temple throne surrounded by singing seraphim—John delivers this earth-shattering apostolic commentary: 'These things Isaiah said because he saw His [Jesus's] glory and spoke of Him' (John 12:41).",
                "Think of the gravity of this statement: in Isaiah 6, the prophet explicitly says, 'My eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts (Yahweh Sabaoth)!' John, writing under the infallible inspiration of the Holy Spirit, reveals that the Person Isaiah saw on that throne was none other than Jesus Christ in His pre-incarnate divine majesty. What the prophet saw, the apostle unlocked!"
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Isaiah 6:5", "'Woe is me, for I am undone! Because I am a man of unclean lips... For my eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts.'", "King, the LORD of hosts", "The supreme vision of Yahweh Sabaoth in the Old Testament."),
                ScriptureVerse("John 12:41", "These things Isaiah said because he saw His glory and spoke of Him.", "Saw His glory", "The Apostle John explicitly states the glory on the throne was Jesus's glory."),
                ScriptureVerse("1 Peter 1:10–11", "...searching what, or what manner of time, the Spirit of Christ who was in them was indicating when He testified beforehand the sufferings of Christ and the glories that would follow.", "Spirit of Christ in them", "Peter confirms it was the Spirit of Christ speaking through the prophets.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "John 12:41 directly equates the Yahweh of Isaiah 6 with Jesus Christ, establishing that the prophet's throne-room vision was a vision of Christ's glory.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Yahweh Sabaoth (יהוה צְבָאוֹת)" to "The LORD of Heavenly Armies / Hosts.",
                "Doxa (δόξα)" to "Glory, radiant divine splendor, and visible majesty.",
                "Eiden (εἶδεν)" to "Literally 'gazed upon / saw with understanding'."
            )
        ),
        BookChapter(
            id = 11,
            partTitle = "Part IV: Comparing What John Saw and What the Prophets Saw",
            title = "Revelation 1 vs. Daniel 7 & 10: The Glorified Christ",
            subtitle = "The Unmistakable Visual Concordance of the Heavenly High Priest",
            keyVerseRef = "Revelation 1:13–15 & Daniel 10:5–6",
            keyVerseText = "His head and hair were white like wool, as white as snow, and His eyes like a flame of fire; His feet were like fine bronze, as if refined in a furnace... and His voice as the sound of many waters.",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "When the Apostle John was exiled on the penal island of Patmos, he was in the Spirit on the Lord's Day. Suddenly he heard a voice like a trumpet behind him. Turning around, he saw seven golden lampstands, and in their midst, One like the Son of Man.",
                "Observe the word-for-word visual harmony with the prophet Daniel. In Daniel 7:9, the hair of the Ancient of Days is 'like pure wool' and His garment 'white as snow.' In Daniel 10:5-6, the divine man is clothed in linen, with a golden belt, 'his eyes like torches of fire, his arms and feet like burnished bronze in color, and the sound of his words like the voice of a multitude.'",
                "In Revelation 1:13-16, John describes Jesus with the identical markers: white hair like wool, eyes like a flame of fire, feet like fine brass, and a voice like the sound of many waters! Both Daniel and John had the exact same physical reaction: Daniel fell with his face to the ground into a deep sleep (Daniel 10:9), and John fell at Jesus's feet as dead (Revelation 1:17). In both cases, the divine Lord placed His right hand upon the seer and said: 'Do not be afraid!'"
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Daniel 10:6", "His body was like beryl, his face like the appearance of lightning, his eyes like torches of fire, his arms and feet like burnished bronze...", "Torches of fire & burnished bronze", "Daniel's vision of the pre-incarnate divine Lord."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 1:14–15", "His head and hair were white like wool, as white as snow, and His eyes like a flame of fire; His feet were like fine bronze...", "White as snow, flame of fire", "John's vision of the glorified Jesus on Patmos."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 1:17–18", "And when I saw Him, I fell at His feet as dead. But He laid His right hand on me, saying to me, 'Do not be afraid; I am the First and the Last.'", "I am the First and the Last", "Jesus asserts the exact sovereign title of God from Isaiah 44:6.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "The visionary description of Jesus in Revelation 1 combines the attributes of Daniel's 'Ancient of Days' and 'Son of Man', affirming His eternal deity.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Chalkolibanon (χαλκολίβανον)" to "Fine glowing or burnished bronze, incandescent with heat.",
                "Phlox pyros (φλὸξ πυρός)" to "Flame of fire — piercing omniscience and divine holiness.",
                "Ho Protos kai ho Eschatos" to "The First and the Last — the eternal beginning and consummation."
            )
        ),
        BookChapter(
            id = 12,
            partTitle = "Part IV: Comparing What John Saw and What the Prophets Saw",
            title = "Revelation 4–5 vs. Ezekiel 1: The Throne Room and the Slain Lamb",
            subtitle = "Universal Adoration Offered Equally to the Creator and to the Lamb",
            keyVerseRef = "Revelation 5:13",
            keyVerseText = "And every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth... I heard saying: 'Blessing and honor and glory and power be to Him who sits on the throne, AND to the Lamb, forever and ever!'",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "Ezekiel chapter 1 and Revelation chapters 4 and 5 represent the two greatest depictions of the celestial throne room in all of Scripture. Ezekiel described four living creatures (the cherubim) with the faces of a lion, an ox, a man, and an eagle, moving beneath a crystal firmament, with a rainbow surrounding the throne (Ezekiel 1:10, 22, 28).",
                "When John was caught up into heaven in Revelation 4, he saw that exact same crystal sea, the exact same emerald rainbow encircling the throne, and the four living creatures crying unceasingly: 'Holy, holy, holy!' But then something cosmic takes place in chapter 5. A scroll sealed with seven seals lies in the right hand of the One on the throne, and no created being in heaven or earth is worthy to open it.",
                "Suddenly, John weeps, but an elder says: 'Do not weep. Behold, the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has prevailed to open the scroll!' When John looks, he sees 'in the midst of the throne and of the four living creatures... a Lamb as though it had been slain' (Revelation 5:6). The Lamb takes the scroll, and immediately all heaven breaks out into a new song, singing: 'Worthy is the Lamb who was slain to receive power and riches and wisdom and strength and honor and glory and blessing!' (Revelation 5:12).",
                "No created angel is ever worshipped in heaven. To give a creature equal worship with God would be supreme idolatry. Yet every creature in heaven, earth, and under the earth gives identical, co-equal adoration 'to Him who sits on the throne AND to the Lamb, forever and ever!' Jesus is in the center of the throne because Jesus is God."
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Ezekiel 1:28", "Like the appearance of a rainbow in a cloud on a rainy day, so was the appearance of the brightness all around it. This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD.", "Rainbow around the throne", "Ezekiel's vision of the radiant divine throne room."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 5:6", "And I looked, and behold, in the midst of the throne and of the four living creatures, and in the midst of the elders, stood a Lamb as though it had been slain...", "In the midst of the throne", "The Lamb shares the very center of the divine throne."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 5:13", "'Blessing and honor and glory and power be to Him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb, forever and ever!'", "To the Lamb forever and ever", "Co-equal sevenfold divine doxology offered to God and the Lamb.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "In Revelation 5, the Lamb occupies the center of the throne seen by Ezekiel and receives the identical worship offered to God Almighty by the entire universe.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Arnion (ἀρνίον)" to "The Lamb — Christ sacrificed as the Passover offering.",
                "En meso tou thronou" to "In the very middle/center of the throne — shared sovereign seat.",
                "Axios (ἄξιος)" to "Worthy — possessing inherent, deserved supreme honor."
