        BookChapter(
            id = 13,
            partTitle = "Part IV: Comparing What John Saw and What the Prophets Saw",
            title = "Zechariah 12 vs. John 19 & Revelation 1: The Pierced God Returns",
            subtitle = "From Golgotha's Spear to the Clouds of Judgment",
            keyVerseRef = "Revelation 1:7",
            keyVerseText = "Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him, even they who pierced Him. And all the tribes of the earth will mourn because of Him. Even so, Amen.",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "When the Roman soldier thrust a spear into Jesus's side on the cross, blood and water flowed out. The Apostle John was standing right there at the foot of the cross, and he wrote: 'He who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true; and he knows that he is telling the truth, so that you may believe... For these things were done that the Scripture should be fulfilled... \"They shall look on Him whom they pierced\"' (John 19:35-37).",
                "John knew Zechariah 12:10 intimately. In Zechariah, Yahweh declared: 'They will look upon ME whom they pierced.' On Mount Calvary, John saw with his own physical eyes the physical piercing of God in the flesh.",
                "Decades later on Patmos, John looks forward to the glorious climax of history in Revelation 1:7: 'Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him, even they who pierced Him.' What Zechariah prophesied in shadow, and what John witnessed at Golgotha, will culminate in the glorious return of Jesus Christ as the pierced, triumphant King of Kings."
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Zechariah 12:10", "They will look upon Me whom they pierced.", "Yahweh speaks", "Prophecy of the divine pierced Redeemer."),
                ScriptureVerse("John 19:34, 37", "One of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear... 'They shall look on Him whom they pierced.'", "Eyewitness fulfillment", "John witnesses the literal physical piercing of Christ."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 1:7", "Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him, even they who pierced Him.", "Coming with clouds", "Universal revelation of the pierced King to all humanity.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "Zechariah, John's Gospel, and Revelation unite in declaring that the One pierced on the tree is Yahweh Himself, returning in glory.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Nuxan (ἔνυξεν)" to "Pierced or pricked deeply with a spear point.",
                "Kopsontai (κόψονται)" to "They will mourn and beat their breasts in deep realization.",
                "Met' nephelon (μετὰ τῶν νεφελῶν)" to "Coming with the clouds of divine presence."
            )
        ),
        BookChapter(
            id = 14,
            partTitle = "Part V: The Ultimate Conclusion",
            title = "The Great Confession: My Lord and My God",
            subtitle = "The Unbroken Biblical Witness from Genesis to Revelation",
            keyVerseRef = "John 20:28",
            keyVerseText = "And Thomas answered and said to Him, 'My Lord and my God!'",
            bodyParagraphs = listOf(
                "The journey through the Holy Scriptures leads to one inescapable summit. We have seen what the Old Testament prophets saw: Isaiah gazing upon the Holy King of Hosts (Isaiah 6), Daniel witnessing the Son of Man receiving divine worship on the clouds (Daniel 7), Ezekiel seeing the likeness of a man upon the glowing sapphire throne (Ezekiel 1), Zechariah hearing Yahweh announce that He Himself would be pierced (Zechariah 12), and Micah proclaiming the Bethlehem Ruler whose origins are from eternity (Micah 5:2).",
                "We have listened to what Jesus said of Himself: 'Before Abraham was, I AM' (John 8:58), 'I and the Father are one' (John 10:30), 'He who has seen Me has seen the Father' (John 14:9), claiming authority to forgive all sins (Mark 2:10), to raise the dead at His command (John 5:28), and proclaiming in the Apocalypse: 'I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End' (Revelation 22:13).",
                "We have seen the Apostle John stand as the bridge between prophecy and fulfillment: revealing that Isaiah saw Jesus's glory (John 12:41), showing Jesus possessing the identical features of the Ancient of Days (Revelation 1), and beholding the Lamb seated upon the singular throne of God forever (Revelation 22:3).",
                "The only appropriate response to this divine reality is the response of the Apostle Thomas. When the resurrected Lord appeared and invited Thomas to touch the nail marks in His hands and the spear wound in His side, Thomas did not construct a philosophical defense. He fell to his knees in adoration and proclaimed: 'My Lord and my God!' (Ho Kyrios mou kai ho Theos mou).",
                "Jesus did not correct him; Jesus commended his faith and pronounced a blessing on every reader who reaches this same truth: 'Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed' (John 20:29). Jesus is not merely a moral teacher, a holy prophet, or an exalted angel. Scripture testifies with one united voice: Jesus Christ is God manifest in the flesh."
            ),
            scriptures = listOf(
                ScriptureVerse("Titus 2:13", "Looking for the blessed hope and glorious appearing of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ...", "Our Great God and Savior", "Granville Sharp's rule of Greek grammar: both 'God' and 'Savior' refer to the single person Jesus Christ."),
                ScriptureVerse("1 John 5:20", "And we know that the Son of God has come and has given us an understanding... His Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God and eternal life.", "This is the true God", "Apostle John's final doctrinal summary in his first epistle."),
                ScriptureVerse("Revelation 22:20", "He who testifies to these things says, 'Surely I am coming quickly.' Amen. Even so, come, Lord Jesus!", "Come, Lord Jesus!", "The final apostolic prayer of the canon of Scripture.")
            ),
            theologicalProofSummary = "From prophetic theophanies to apostolic visions, the Bible speaks with one unified voice: Jesus Christ is Lord, Savior, and God Almighty.",
            originalLanguageNotes = listOf(
                "Ho Kyrios mou kai ho Theos mou" to "The Lord of me and the God of me — Thomas's supreme confession.",
                "Tou megalou Theou kai Soteros hemon" to "Our Great God and Savior (Titus 2:13) — single referent.",
                "Maranatha (μαράνα θά)" to "Aramaic prayer: 'Our Lord, come!'"
            )
        )
    )
